    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d107">

                <pb facs="0086" n="78" sameAs="frus:frus1939v05/pg78"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">862.852/45</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum of Conversation, by the Assistant Secretary
                        of State</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Grady</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washbstgton</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1939-11-03">November 3, 1939</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <table>
                    <row role="label">
                        <cell rendition="#nowrap #top">Participants:</cell>
                        <cell rendition="#hanging-indent">Mr. Daniel P. McDonald, Counselor at Law,
                            New York City</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell/>
                        <cell rendition="#hanging-indent">Mr. Henry F. Grady</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell/>
                        <cell rendition="#hanging-indent">Mr. William R. Vallance<anchor n="26"
                                xml:id="d107-f1"/>
                        </cell>
                    </row>
                </table>
                <p>Mr. McDonald called on me this morning, at the suggestion of Mr. Saugstad<anchor
                        n="27" xml:id="d107-f2"/> and Mr. Vallance. Mr. McDonald said that he
                    represented interests in New York who wish to buy the German ships interned in
                    Latin American ports. He had approached the German Embassy and had been given
                    encouragement. He had attempted to see Sir Owen Chalkley<anchor n="28"
                        xml:id="d107-f3"/> at the British Embassy but it was intimated that he could
                    not discuss matters of this kind with any one in authority unless he were
                    introduced through officials in the Department of State. He asked me if I could
                    arrange for an appointment for him. He said the purpose of the purchase was to
                    put the ships into the trans-Atlantic trade for carrying cargo to the British
                    and French and that the ships would be registered under the Panama flag. I told
                    him that I would give thought to the matter and let him know.</p>
                <p>After consulting with Mr. Moffat<anchor n="29" xml:id="d107-f4"/> and Mr.
                        Hackworth,<anchor n="30" xml:id="d107-f5"/> I telephoned Mr. McDonald at his
                    hotel and advised him that I would be unable to arrange an introduction to Sir
                    Owen Chalkley.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>H[<hi rendition="#small-caps">enry</hi>] F. G[<hi
                            rendition="#small-caps">rady</hi>]</signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
